Unfortunately it's not correct. After the unclean shutdown, there is no guarantee the state/trienode history can be fully recovered. A few items near the head may be truncated by the freezer itself.

If the corresponding history entries are not fully present, then there is no way to unindex. It's the main reason we choose to silently wait until the the chain catches up again. 


The concern is valid, as described in the comment. In practice it's very unlikely to happen, as we only persist the states with at least 128 confirmations on top.
```
// As a workaround, indexIniter waits until the missing histories are regenerated
// by chain recovery, under the assumption that the recovered histories will be
// identical to the lost ones. Fork-awareness should be added in the future to
// correctly handle histories affected by reorgs.
```

